Durarara!! AU
Chapter 30
They did not wait at the light for long.
Curtis had said he was right behind them. Then the man in the fur-trimmed coat stepped into the gap, and the first-year appeared like a problem that had been following the noise. Rio took one look at the way the three of them stood and decided the sidewalk no longer belonged to students.
“We’re not standing here like decorations,” she said.
Anri followed without argument. The torn Raira jacket, the pompadour in a bin, the slap still hanging in the air — none of it felt finished, but Curtis had already put his body between them and the next bad decision. Walking away was the only useful thing left.
Half a block later the city noise thinned enough for talking.
Rio spoke first.
“…You met him the night he got here too.”
Anri’s steps did not change. Her voice stayed soft.
“I saw him. I didn’t meet him until the next day.”
Rio glanced sideways.
“The street. The band. The mask.”
“Yes.”
A small silence. Then Rio exhaled through her nose.
“I met him before the songs.”
Anri looked at her properly for the first time since the gate.
Rio kept her eyes forward.
“I was on a roof. Someone was supposed to jump with me. He laughed instead. So I jumped alone.” She said it flat, the way people say things they have already survived once. “Curtis caught me. Cracked the wall doing it. Talked to me like my reason wasn’t something to mock. Didn’t ask for a thank-you. Didn’t even give me his name.”
Anri’s fingers tightened on her bag strap.
“…I didn’t know.”
“Most people don’t. He promised he wouldn’t tell anyone. He kept it.” Rio’s mouth pulled sideways. “Then I saw him again an hour later singing about kids who got off ledges and masks that stay on. Hood fell. Braids came out. Next morning he was asleep in our classroom like none of it happened.”
Anri was quiet for several steps.
“I only had the performance,” she said eventually. “The voice. The way he stayed inside the mask even when people started watching. When the hood dropped I… remembered the shape of him. The next day he introduced himself with almost nothing. Just the name. Then he accidentally volunteered as class rep with me.”
A faint, almost private breath.
“After class I asked if it was him. He admitted it. I told him I liked the mask. And that his face was nice too.” She looked briefly embarrassed by the memory. “I bowed and ran away. It was the first time I had said something that direct to anyone.”
Rio’s expression flickered — not quite jealousy, not quite understanding.
“He’s like that. Says the heavy thing like it’s ordinary. Then walks off before you know what to do with it.”
“Yes.”
They walked a little farther. The light changed somewhere behind them. Neither turned back to check whether Curtis had finished with Izaya and the first-year.
Rio spoke again, quieter.
“He hugged me on the stairs the first day. Told me there was only one version of him. That he would save me again. That I mattered.” Her voice stayed steady, but the edges were careful. “I still don’t know what to do with that. Most people either hover or disappear. He just… decided what I needed and did it.”
Anri’s answer came after a longer pause.
“When the bullies cornered me, he walked between us like it was already finished. Called them not his type. Took a knife. Cut hair. Got hit with a board and stood back up. Izaya stabbed him and he complained about the shirt.” She almost smiled, then didn’t. “He keeps putting himself in the way. Then apologizes for being late to class.”
Rio looked at her.
“You’re soft on him.”
Anri did not deny it.
“So are you.”
Another stretch of sidewalk. A vending machine hummed. Somewhere a train passed overhead.
Rio’s voice dropped.
“I was the first one he caught. You’re the one he keeps walking home. I don’t know what that makes any of us.”
Anri’s reply was almost too soft to hear over the city.
“It makes him the same person both times.”
They stopped at the next corner without agreeing to. Neither said they were waiting. Both of them were.
Behind them, the gate and the trash bin and the information broker were already becoming someone else’s problem.
In front of them, the street kept moving like Ikebukuro always did — loud, indifferent, and full of people who had never been caught mid-air or told their face was nice by a girl who usually said nothing at all.
Rio folded her arms.
“If he gets stabbed again I’m going to be angry.”
Anri nodded once.
“If he disappears again… I’m going to look.”
Neither of them said the third thing.
That they were already looking.
Even while they walked away.
Rio didn’t look at her when she said it.
“Hey, Anri-chan… what’s it like when Curtis says your name with -chan in it.” A short pause. “He doesn’t do that for anyone else. Except you.”
Anri’s steps slowed for half a beat.
The city kept moving around them — scooter, vending machine, someone laughing too loud outside a convenience store — but the question sat in the space between them like it had weight.
“…I noticed,” Anri said eventually. Her voice stayed soft. “I didn’t think anyone else had.”
Rio’s arms stayed folded.
“Hard not to. It’s different. When he says my name it’s just… Rio. Or Rio-kun when he’s being careful. With you it’s automatic. Like he already decided.”
Anri’s fingers adjusted the strap of her bag. The motion was small, the kind she used when she needed something to do with her hands.
“It feels…” She searched for the word and didn’t find a clean one. “Warm. And a little dangerous. Not because he means anything bad. Because he says it like it’s already settled. Like the distance most people keep is optional with me.”
She glanced at Rio, then away again.
“When he first started, I thought it was just him being foreign. The way he talks. Then I heard him with everyone else. Kadota. Masaomi. Even you. And it stayed different.”
Rio was quiet for a few steps.
“Does it make you feel special.”
It wasn’t quite a question. It wasn’t quite an accusation either.
Anri didn’t pretend.
“…Yes.” She exhaled, almost embarrassed by the honesty. “It also makes me feel like I have to be careful not to lean on it too much. He’s already carrying a lot of people’s weight. I don’t want to become another thing he has to manage.”
Rio’s mouth pulled sideways.
“That’s the problem with him. He makes you feel like the only one and then walks off to go catch someone else.”
Anri’s answer came quieter.
“He called you Rio the night he saved you. Before he even knew if you’d stay. That means something too.”
Rio didn’t argue.
They walked another half block in silence. Somewhere behind them the gate and the trash bin and the information broker were still sorting themselves out. Neither girl turned around.
Rio spoke again, almost to the sidewalk.
“I asked because I wanted to know if it felt the same from the inside. Hearing it.”
Anri’s reply was barely above the city noise.
“It feels like he’s choosing to see me. Even when I try to stay small.”
Rio’s arms loosened a fraction.
“…Yeah. That’s exactly it.”
Neither of them said the rest out loud.
That both of them had already started listening for their own version of it.
Rio didn’t ease into it.
“You ever wonder if he thinks about us in other ways.”
Anri’s ears went warm before the rest of her caught up. The strap of her bag suddenly needed both hands.
“By other ways… do you mean… s-sexually?”
Rio giggled. Soft. Not mean. Just delighted that Anri had said the word out loud.
“Yeah, Anri-chan.”
Anri’s face stayed pink. She looked at the sidewalk like it might offer a cleaner answer.
“I’m actually not sure. He doesn’t seem to chase girls. And his mind doesn’t seem to go in that direction.” She hesitated, then added, quieter, “When he looks at people it’s like he’s… reading them. Not undressing them.”
Rio hummed.
“Yeah. He did say he had an ex back in L.A. Other than that he’s very vague on her. Except that he really doesn’t like her. Or miss her. Remotely.” A small shrug. “You get the feeling the story ends with him being done, not heartbroken.”
Anri nodded once, still pink.
Rio’s mouth tilted.
“Your friend Masaomi, on the other hand, is a bit of a pig. He was talking with Mikado and made a pass at me on the first day.” She said it without heat, more like a fact she had already filed. “Curtis must have heard as well. I think even though he sleeps in class… he’s possibly listening to everything around him.”
Anri’s blush cooled into something more thoughtful.
“…He called him that once. A flirty pig. After the hallway. When Masaomi tried to lecture him about jumping in.” She remembered the way Curtis had stood there unphased while Masaomi’s fist found something that didn’t move. “He doesn’t miss much. Even when his eyes are closed.”
Rio glanced at her.
“Does that bother you? That he might hear more than he pretends to?”
Anri considered it.
“No. It makes me careful with what I say near him. But it also makes me feel… safer. Like if something is wrong, he already knows the shape of it.” She looked down. “The other part. The… sexual part. I don’t think he looks at me that way. Or at you. He looks at people like he’s deciding whether they’re going to break.”
Rio was quiet for a few steps.
“Maybe that’s why the -chan feels heavy. He’s not trying to get anything. He’s just… placing you closer than the rest.”
Anri’s voice went small.
“And you wonder if closer means something else.”
Rio didn’t deny it.
“I wonder if he even knows. He talks about his body like it’s a tool. Scars. Fighting. Not… wanting. The ex is the only proof he ever did, and he talks about her like a closed door.”
Anri’s fingers worried the strap again.
“If he does think about it… I don’t think he’d say. He’s careful with other people’s weight. He already thinks he’s too much for normal rooms.”
Rio’s smile came back, thinner.
“So we’re left guessing while he sleeps through math and still hears Masaomi being a pig.”
Anri almost smiled.
“Yes.”
They walked another block. The gate was gone behind them. The man in the fur coat and the first-year and the ripped jacket were someone else’s problem for a few more minutes.
Rio spoke once more, lighter on purpose.
“For the record… if he ever did look at either of us that way… I’d want to know. Not because I’m ready. Because I hate not being able to read him the way he reads everyone else.”
Anri’s answer was barely above the traffic.
“…Me too.”
Neither of them said the rest.
That wondering about it already meant the answer mattered.
The sidewalk conversation was still sitting between them — first nights, -chan, an L.A. door that stayed shut — when Anri’s steps faltered.
It was not a sound from the street.
It was a voice she already knew how to hold still for.
Anri-chan. Get Rio out of here. Don’t let her look back. If she sees Izaya and he opens his mouth about Mika… that’s too many people who know. Please, Anri-chan. Rio hates Izaya.
Curtis. Not from behind them. From inside the quiet place she used for another voice she did not advertise.
Anri’s fingers tightened on the bag strap.
The plan from the hotel night lined up in one clean stack. He had found Mika. He had asked her for Mikado’s number. He had told her Yagiri, Shizuo, the Black Rider. He had asked her not to come to the room. She had sent the number anyway. She had said I trust you. She had said don’t let them take her again.
This was that same ask. Just on a sidewalk instead of a screen.
She did not look back.
She did not say Mika.
She turned to Rio like the next block had already been decided.
“Rio. We’re leaving.”
Rio blinked, thrown out of the softer talk.
“…We just started walking. He’s still—” Her chin started to lift toward the gate. “If that’s Izaya—”
“Don’t.”
Anri’s voice stayed soft. It did not stay optional.
“Please don’t look. If you look, he will talk. If he talks, it becomes a crowd. Curtis asked me to take you.” A small breath. “I already said I would help him with this. I need you to come with me and not turn around.”
Rio’s mouth went tight.
“So he gets the flea-market coat and we get sent away like children.”
“No.” Anri shook her head once. “You get sent away because you hate that man, and he uses hate like a microphone. Curtis knows that. I know that.” She stepped so she was slightly in Rio’s line if Rio tried the street anyway. “I am not leaving him. I am doing the part of the plan I already agreed to.”
Rio searched her face.
“…You knew something was going to happen.”
Anri did not lie.
“I knew he found someone who should not be a rumor. I knew Izaya does not get to be in that sentence. That is all I can say here.” Her voice dropped. “Please, Rio. Walk. If you look back and he says a name, we cannot put it back.”
Rio’s fists curled.
She wanted the gate. She wanted the man in the fur coat to have a bad afternoon. She wanted Curtis not to stand there alone with Mikado and a ripped jacket.
She also heard please the way Curtis said it when he meant the city was about to get expensive.
“…Fine.” She started walking, faster, angry at the obedience. “But if Izaya puts a hand on him, I’m coming back. Plan or not.”
“I know,” Anri said.
She fell in beside her and did not look.
In the private channel, small, the same girl who had typed I’m coming and then stayed away because he asked:
I have her. We won’t look. Don’t let him say her name. I’m still with you. Just not on that sidewalk.
A thinner beat, almost embarrassed that the voice in her head had used -chan the same way the street did.
Be careful, Curtis-kun. Please.
On a corner that still wanted a fight,
Anri Sonohara closed the loop
he had opened in a hotel.
Rio Kamichika walked without turning
and hated every step.
Behind them, Izaya still had Mikado
and a ripped jacket.
In front of them, Mika Harima
stayed a name
that did not get
to become a crowd.
The private channel stayed open just long enough for him to spend it.
Anri-chan. Thank you. I owe you big time. Thanks even after… well. For the ugly parts of myself that still make you uneasy. I embarrassed you again by showing the crowd I’m a freak. The way I handled Takeshi.
Anri’s mouth opened on a breath she did not get to use.
The link snapped.
Not like a door. Like a string pulled past its length. One more corner, one more stretch of sidewalk, and the voice that had been sitting behind her ribs was just city noise again.
She stopped walking without meaning to.
Rio turned.
“Anri-chan? You okay?”
Anri blinked. The street came back in pieces — vending machine, Rio’s bag strap, the fact that she had not looked behind them and still felt like she had left something important on the pavement.
“…Yes.” Too fast. She corrected it. “No. I’m— I’m fine. He just…” She stopped. Rio could not have the channel. Rio could not have Mika. “He asked me to keep moving. Then he said thank you. And then it was gone.”
Rio’s eyes narrowed.
“Gone. Like a text.”
Anri shook her head once. The motion was small. Honest enough to be a problem if she kept going.
“Like he was closer than he should have been. Then he wasn’t.” She pressed her fingers to the strap until the leather creaked. “He thinks he embarrassed me. At the gate. With Takeshi. He called himself a freak again.”
Rio’s face hardened.
“That’s what he took from that? Not the ripped jacket. Not Youko. He took your face.”
Anri’s ears went warm. Not from the word sexually this time. From the memory of a crowd and a spine she had been afraid of, and the way he had walked away from her stare like it was a verdict.
“I didn’t mean it as a verdict,” she said, quiet. “I was scared of how easy it was for him. That isn’t the same as thinking he’s a monster. He keeps filing it like I agreed with the crowd.”
Rio glanced once toward the direction they were not allowed to look, then forced herself forward.
“So he thanks you for dragging me off, apologizes for existing too loud, and drops the call before you can tell him he’s an idiot.” A thin breath. “Yeah. That tracks.”
Anri started walking again. Slower.
“I was going to say it wasn’t ugly to me. Not like that. I was going to say I stayed.” Her voice thinned. “He didn’t hear it.”
Rio bumped her shoulder, not gentle, not cruel.
“Then you say it when he catches up. Out loud. Where a crowd can’t steal his translation.” She watched Anri’s face. “You’re pale. If this is him pulling you into another disappearing act, I want the version I am allowed to hate.”
Anri almost smiled. It didn’t hold.
“It isn’t a disappearing act. It’s the plan. The part where fewer people know a name.” She looked at Rio, steady now. “I’m okay. I just didn’t get to answer him. That feels worse than the gate.”
Rio muttered something that might have been idiot and might have been Curtis.
They kept walking.
Behind them, a mind-link had run out of distance.
In Anri Sonohara’s chest, an unpaid sentence sat unfinished:
You’re not a freak to me.
She would have to say it later.
With her mouth.
Before he filed the silence as proof.
Rio’s phone buzzed.
She almost didn’t look. Then she saw the name and the shortness of it, and her thumb was already on the thread.
Curtis: sorry rio. ill make it up to you.
Rio stopped under a shop awning long enough to type with both thumbs, jaw tight.
Rio: you better.
Rio: and he better not stab you again.
Rio: anri-chan told me what happened last time between you two.
She hit send before she could sand it down.
Anri’s eyes flicked to the screen, then away, guilty in a small, specific way.
“…I didn’t tell her to use it as a threat.”
“It isn’t a threat.” Rio locked the phone and started walking again. “It’s a receipt. He got stabbed twice and complained about a shirt. Izaya smiled through it. I’m not doing a second version of that story where Curtis texts sorry from a sidewalk and we find out later there was blood in the coat.”
Anri’s voice stayed soft.
“He asked us to leave so there wouldn’t be a second version.”
“I know.” Rio’s mouth pulled. “That’s why I walked. That’s not why I have to like the text.” She glanced at Anri. “You told me because you were scared. Not because you wanted me to be nice about the flea-market coat.”
Anri nodded once.
The phone buzzed again in Rio’s pocket. She didn’t check it yet. She let the last message sit with him — you better, and the stabbing, and Anri’s name attached to the warning like a witness.
“If he makes it up,” Rio said, quieter, “it better not be another song and a disappearing act. I want him in one piece. I want Izaya’s mouth shut. I want Anri-chan to stop going pale in the middle of the street.”
Anri’s ears warmed.
“I’m not pale because of Izaya.”
“I know that too,” Rio said.
They walked.
On a sidewalk they had been ordered not to turn around on,
Rio Kamichika collected an IOU
and sent a scar back
as a condition.
Curtis Nightstar had said sorry.
She had said don’t bleed for him and meant come back.
